1. What is Excel?
Definition:
 Microsoft Excel is a spreadsheet software developed by Microsoft that is used to store, organize, calculate, and analyze data in a tabular format.
2. Uses of Excel
Excel is used in almost every field because it handles numbers and data efficiently.
Major Uses:
●	Data entry and record keeping

●	Calculations using formulas

●	Data analysis

●	Creating charts and graphs

●	Financial tasks (budgets, salary sheets)

●	Reporting

Examples:
●	A company tracks employee salaries in Excel.

●	A shop owner maintains daily sales records.

●	A student creates a monthly expense sheet.

3. Excel for Data Visualization & Analysis
A. Data Analysis
Excel helps us understand data by:
●	Sorting (arranging data A–Z, low to high)

●	Filtering (showing only needed data)

●	Using formulas (SUM, AVERAGE, COUNT, IF, etc.)

●	Pivot Tables (summarizing large data)

Example:
 From a list of 100 students’ marks, Excel can quickly find:
●	Class average

●	Highest and lowest marks

●	Number of students who passed

B. Data Visualization
Excel can turn numbers into charts and graphs.
Types of charts:
●	Bar chart

●	Column chart

●	Pie chart

●	Line chart

Example:
 Monthly sales data can be shown as a bar graph to see which month had the highest sales.
4. Installation of Excel
Excel comes as part of Microsoft Office / Microsoft 365.
Basic Steps:
1.	Go to the official Microsoft website.

2.	Purchase or sign in with Microsoft 365 account.

3.	Download the Office setup file.

4.	Run the installer and follow instructions.

5.	After installation, open Excel from Start Menu.

5. Excel Interface & Attributes
When you open Excel, you see many parts. These help you work easily.
Row
●	A horizontal line of cells.

●	Numbered as 1, 2, 3…

Example: Row 1 may contain column headings like Name, Age, Marks.
Row Header
●	The numbers on the left side (1, 2, 3…).

●	They identify each row.
Column
●	A vertical line of cells.

●	Named A, B, C…

Example: Column A → Student Names
 Column B → Marks
Column Header
●	The letters at the top (A, B, C…).

●	They identify each column.


Cell
●	The intersection of a row and column.

●	Each cell has an address.

Example: Cell B2 means Column B and Row 2.

Active Cell
●	The cell that is currently selected.

●	It has a bold border.

Example: When you click on C3, it becomes the active cell where you type data.

Ribbon
●	The strip at the top of Excel containing tools and commands.

●	Helps in formatting, formulas, charts, etc.


Ribbon Tabs
Different sections of the Ribbon:
●	Home

●	Insert

●	Page Layout

●	Formulas

●	Data

●	Review

●	View

Example: To insert a chart → Go to Insert tab.

Quick Access Toolbar (QAT)
●	Small toolbar at the top-left corner.

●	Contains frequently used commands like Save, Undo, Redo.

●	Can be customized.

