Numerical Functions in Excel
These functions are mainly used to perform calculations and data analysis.

1. SUM
Definition: Adds a group of numbers.
Syntax:
 =SUM(range)
Example:
 Marks in A1:A5 → 50, 60, 70, 80, 90
 =SUM(A1:A5) → 350

2. COUNT
Definition: Counts cells that contain numbers only.
Syntax:
 =COUNT(range)
Example:
 A1=10, A2=Text, A3=25
 =COUNT(A1:A3) → 2

3. MAX
Definition: Returns the largest number in a range.
Example:
 =MAX(A1:A5) → Highest mark

4. MIN
Definition: Returns the smallest number in a range.
Example:
 =MIN(A1:A5) → Lowest mark

5. AVERAGE
Definition: Finds the mean (average) of numbers.
Formula:
 =AVERAGE(A1:A5)
If total = 350 and 5 students → 70

6. RANK
Definition: Gives the position of a number in a list.
Syntax:
 =RANK(number, range)
Example:
 Marks: 90, 80, 70
 =RANK(80, A1:A3) → 2

7. COUNTIF
Definition: Counts cells that meet one condition.
Syntax:
 =COUNTIF(range, criteria)
Example:
 Count students scoring above 50
 =COUNTIF(A1:A10, ">50")

8. COUNTIFS
Definition: Counts cells with multiple conditions.
Example:
 =COUNTIFS(A1:A10, ">50", B1:B10, "Pass")

9. SUMIF
Definition: Adds numbers based on one condition.
Example:
 Sum marks of students who passed
 =SUMIF(B1:B10, "Pass", A1:A10)

10. SUMIFS
Definition: Adds numbers using multiple conditions.
Example:
 =SUMIFS(A1:A10, B1:B10, "Pass", C1:C10, "Section A")

11. AVERAGEIF
Definition: Average based on one condition.
Example:
 =AVERAGEIF(B1:B10, "Pass", A1:A10)

12. AVERAGEIFS
Definition: Average with multiple conditions.
Example:
 =AVERAGEIFS(A1:A10, B1:B10, "Pass", C1:C10, "Section A")

13. COUNTA
Definition: Counts non-empty cells (text, numbers, symbols).
Example:
 A1=10, A2=Text, A3=blank
 =COUNTA(A1:A3) → 2

14. COUNTBLANK
Definition: Counts empty cells.
Example:
 =COUNTBLANK(A1:A10)

15. ROUND
Definition: Rounds a number to a specified number of digits.
Syntax:
 =ROUND(number, digits)
Example:
 =ROUND(45.678, 2) → 45.68

16. ROUNDUP
Definition: Always rounds up.
Example:
 =ROUNDUP(45.123, 2) → 45.13

17. ROUNDDOWN
Definition: Always rounds down.
Example:
 =ROUNDDOWN(45.987, 2) → 45.98
TEXT FUNCTIONS
Used to clean, extract, and combine text.

1. UPPER
Converts text to uppercase.
=UPPER(text)
Example:
 A1 = "srusti"
 =UPPER(A1) → ROSS

2. LOWER
Converts text to lowercase.
=LOWER(A1) → ross

3. PROPER
Capitalizes the first letter of each word.
=PROPER("data analytics") → Data Analytics

4. LEFT
Extracts text from the left side.
=LEFT(text, number_of_characters)
A1 = "Excel2026"
 =LEFT(A1,5) → Excel

5. RIGHT
Extracts text from the right side.
=RIGHT(A1,4) → 2026

6. MID
Extracts text from the middle.
=MID(text, start_position, number_of_characters)
=MID("SRUSTI",2,3) → RUS

7. CONCAT / CONCATENATE / &
Joins text.
●	=CONCAT(A1,B1)

●	=A1&B1

●	(Old version) =CONCATENATE(A1,B1)

A1="Data", B1="Science" → DataScience

8. TEXTJOIN
Joins text with a separator.
=TEXTJOIN(" ", TRUE, A1:A3)
A1=Excel, A2=Data, A3=Analysis
 → Excel Data Analysis

9. TRIM
Removes extra spaces.
=TRIM(" Excel Class ") → Excel Class

10. SUBSTITUTE
Replaces specific text.
=SUBSTITUTE("2025 Report","2025","2026")
 → 2026 Report

 DATE FUNCTIONS

1. TODAY
Returns current date.
=TODAY() → 11-02-2026

2. NOW
Returns date + time.
=NOW()

3. YEAR
Extracts year.
=YEAR("11-02-2026") → 2026

4. MONTH
=MONTH(A1) → Month number

5. DAY
=DAY(A1) → Day number

6. DAYS
Difference between two dates.
=DAYS("11-02-2026","01-02-2026") → 10

7. DAYS360
Calculates days based on 360-day year (used in finance).

LOGICAL FUNCTIONS

1. IF
Performs a logical test.
=IF(condition, true_value, false_value)
Marks in A1
 =IF(A1>=40,"Pass","Fail")

2. AND
All conditions must be TRUE.
=AND(A1>40,B1="Yes")

3. OR
Any one condition TRUE.

4. IFERROR
Shows alternative result if formula gives error.
=IFERROR(A1/B1,"Error")
5. IFNA
Handles only #N/A errors.

6. IFS
Multiple conditions.
=IFS(A1>=90,"A",A1>=75,"B",A1>=50,"C")
LOOKUP FUNCTIONS
Used to search data in tables.

1. VLOOKUP
Searches vertically.
=VLOOKUP(value, table, column_number, FALSE)
Find marks using roll number.

2. HLOOKUP
Searches horizontally.

3. INDEX
Returns value from a position.
=INDEX(A1:C5,2,3)

4. MATCH
Finds position.
=MATCH("Srusti",A1:A10,0)

5. INDEX + MATCH
Advanced lookup, more flexible than VLOOKUP.

6. LOOKUP
Older function, approximate match.

7. XLOOKUP
Modern replacement of VLOOKUP.
=XLOOKUP(value, lookup_array, return_array)
DATA VALIDATION
Definition:
Data Validation is a feature in Excel that controls what type of data can be entered in a cell. It helps avoid mistakes.

1. Whole Numbers
Allows only integers.
Example:
Marks should be between 0 and 100.
Setting → Whole Number → Between → 0 and 100
If a student enters 150 → Excel shows error.

2. Decimals
Allows decimal numbers.
Example:
Percentage field → 0.00 to 100.00

3. List (Dropdown List)
A. Single List
Creates a dropdown menu.
Example:
Field: Gender
List → Male, Female, Other

B. Dependent List
Second dropdown depends on first.
Example:
First list → Country (India, USA)
Second list → States change based on country selected.

4. Date
Restricts to valid dates.
Example:
Project start date must be after 01-01-2026.

5. Time
Restricts time entries.
Example:
Office timing: Only 9:00 AM – 6:00 PM allowed.

CONDITIONAL FORMATTING
Definition:
Automatically changes cell formatting based on conditions.

1. Highlight Cell Rules
Highlights cells meeting conditions.
Examples:
●	Greater than 80 → Green
●	Less than 40 → Red

2. Top/Bottom Rules
Finds top or bottom values.
Examples:
●	Top 5 students
●	Bottom 10% sales

3. Data Bars
Shows bars inside cells to compare values visually.
Example:
Higher marks → longer bar.

4. Color Scales
Applies gradient colors.
Example:
High sales → green
Low sales → red

5. Icon Sets
Uses symbols (arrows, traffic lights).
Example:
↑ Profit increase
↓ Profit decrease

